ear, half of the works received at most 24% of the citations expected for similar works worldwide (FWCI 1 = world average).","Above the world average: 6%–20% of the works per year have an FWCI above 1, most in 2020 (20%).","Not cited: on average 55% of these works have FWCI 0, meaning no citations within their four-year window. That is why many boxes start at 0.","Mean versus median: the mean is above the median in 4 of 4 years, because a few highly cited works pull the average up. The widest gap is 2020: mean 0.94, median 0.24, top work 4.83. The median is the better guide to a typical work.","Still counting: works from 2023–2025 have not completed their four-year citation window, so their values will rise. Do not compare them with older years yet.","Small base: the values for 2018 and 2020–2025 are based on only 10–29 works (lighter box or hollow point), so read them with care.","Few works: 2017, 2019 and 2026 have fewer than 10 works (2017: 4, 2019: 6, 2026: 8), too few for a box. Each of those works is drawn as a dot."],"citations":["Typical work: works published 2018–2023 have a median of 0–2 citations each; the highest median (2) is in 2018.","Most cited works: the top 10% of works published in 2020 have at least 9.4 citations each, the highest P90 in the chart.","Not yet cited: more than half of the works published 2024–2025 have no citations so far. This is expected for recent works and is not a sign of low quality.","Mean versus median: the mean is above the median in 3 of 5 years, because a few highly cited works pull the average up. The widest gap is 2020: mean 3.6, median 1, top work 22. The median is the better guide to a typical work.","Recent years: works from 2024–2025 have had less than three years to be cited, so their values will rise.","Small base: the values for 2018 and 2020–2025 are based on only 10–29 works (lighter box or hollow point), so read them with care.","Few works: 2017, 2019 and 2026 have fewer than 10 works (2017: 4, 2019: 6, 2026: